===History of revisions===

* Overture – The original overture, used in all stage productions up to 1946 is based chiefly on the deleted song "[[Mis'ry's Comin' Aroun]]", as Kern wanted to save this song in some form. The song was restored in the Prince 1994 revival of the show. The overture also contains fragments of "Ol' Man River", "Can't Help Lovin' Dat Man", and a faster arrangement of "Why Do I Love You?" The overtures for the 1946 revival and the 1966 [[Lincoln Center]] revival consist of medleys of songs from the show. All three overtures were arranged by the show's orchestrator, [[Robert Russell Bennett]], who orchestrated most of Kern's later shows.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "Cotton Blossom" – This number is included in all the stage productions, and shorter versions were used in the 1936 and 1951 film versions.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "Where's the Mate for Me?" – Included in all stage versions, partially sung in the 1936 film version, and sung complete in the 1951 film version.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "[[Make Believe (Jerome Kern song)|Make Believe]]" – Included in all stage versions, and in the 1936 and 1951 film versions.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "Life Upon the Wicked Stage" – Included in all stage versions and the 1951 film version but heard in the 1936 film only in the orchestral score.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "Till Good Luck Comes My Way" – Cut from the 1946 revival but reinstated in the 1971 London revival. It is performed instrumentally in the 1936 film.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "I Might Fall Back on You" – Usually cut from 1946 until reinstated in 1966. Included in the 1951 film.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "C'mon Folks (Queenie's Ballyhoo)" – While included in all of the Broadway productions,{{sfn|Dietz|2019|page=430-431}}{{sfn|Dietz|2018|page=201-202}}{{sfn|Dietz|2015|page=312-313}}{{sfn|Dietz|2016a|pages=171-172}} it was cut from the original [[West End theatre|West End]] production after [[Alberta Hunter]]'s show-stopping performance of the song caused jealousy among her co-stars.{{sfn|Decker|2013|page=129}} It was sung in the prologue to the 1929 film version,{{sfn|Barrios|1995|page=90}} and performed instrumentally in the 1951 film.
* "Olio Dance" – Usually cut, as it was composed to cover a change of scenery; this orchestral piece partially uses the melody of "I Might Fall Back on You".{{citation needed|date=November 2024}} The 1936 film substituted "Gallivantin' Around", performed as an [[Olio (musical number)|olio]] by [[Irene Dunne]] (as Magnolia) in blackface.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}} Some modern productions move the song "I Might Fall Back on You" to this spot.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "You Are Love" – Included in all stage productions, and shorter versions were used in both the 1936 and the 1951 films.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "Act I Finale" – This was shortened in the 1936 film. Its midsection, banjo-dominant, buck-and-wing dance theme became a repeating motif in the 1951 film, played onstage during the backstage miscegenation scene, and later as a soft-shoe dance for Cap'n Andy and granddaughter Kim.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "At the Chicago World's Fair" – Used in all stage productions except for the Prince 1994 revival; an instrumental version was performed in the 1936 film.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "Why Do I Love You?" – Used in all stage versions, the song was originally written as a duet for Magnolia and Ravenal but was re-shaped as a solo for Parthy in the 1994 Broadway revival.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}} A reprise of the song sung by the adult Kim in act 2 has often been omitted or replaced subsequent to the original Broadway production. Songs used as replacements include "Dance Away the Night" and "Nobody Else But Me".{{sfn|Block|1997|page=W37-W41}} "Why Do I Love You?" was sung during the exit music to the 1929 film;{{sfn|Mordden|2015|page=34}} it was performed only as background music for the 1936 film{{sfn|Block|2023|page=32}} and was sung in the 1951 film version.
* "[[In Dahomey#Song from Show Boat|In Dahomey]]" – Cut from the score after the 1946 Broadway production and never revived as it is viewed as racially offensive and unnecessary to the plot. Sung at the [[World's Columbian Exposition|Chicago World's Fair]] by a group of supposedly African natives who chant in a supposed African language before switching to English.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "Goodbye, My Lady Love" – Used only in American productions and the 1936 film.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "[[After the Ball (song)|After the Ball]]" – Included in all stage productions and in both the 1936 and 1951 films.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "Hey, Feller" – Used in the 1927 and 1932 Broadway productions but cut from the 1928 London production and the 1946 Broadway revival{{sfn|Block|1997|page=W37-W41}} and the Prince in the 1994 revival.{{sfn|Lewis|2015|pages=176-177}} The song was sung in the prologue to the 1929 film.{{sfn|Barrios|1995|page=90}} Used as background score in the 1951 film during the opening post-credits scene.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
Additional numbers sometimes included in films and revivals:
*"Mis'ry's Comin' Aroun" was published in the complete vocal score; fragments of it are heard in the score, notably in the original overture and the miscegenation scene.{{sfn|Block|1997|pages=22–24}} It was included in the 1994 [[Harold Prince]] revival.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "Let's Start the New Year" – Included in the 1989 Paper Mill Playhouse production.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "Dance Away the Night" was written by Kern and Hammerstein for the 1928 West End production.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "I Have the Room Above Her" was written by Kern and Hammerstein for Ravenal and Magnolia in the 1936 film. It was included in the 1994 Broadway revival.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "Gallivantin' Around" was written by Kern and Hammerstein for Magnolia for the 1936 film.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "Ah Still Suits Me" was written by Kern and Hammerstein for the 1936 film, sung by Joe and Queenie (Paul Robeson and [[Hattie McDaniel]]).{{citation needed|date=November 2024}} Included in the 1989 Paper Mill Playhouse production.
* "[[Nobody Else but Me]]" was written by Kern and Hammerstein for the 1946 Broadway revival. This was the last song Kern ever wrote; he died shortly before the revival opened. In the 1971 London revival, the song was sung by Julie, in a new scene written for that production.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}} It was frequently heard in revivals until about the 1980s.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}
* "Dandies on Parade" is a dance number arranged for the 1994 Broadway production by David Krane, largely from Kern's music.{{citation needed|date=November 2024}}

